artifacts/paper_draft/method.tex-11-Our goal is twofold: (1)~estimate $\theta_j^{\text{clean}}$, the ability each model would exhibit had it never encountered test items during training, and (2)~diagnose contamination effects at domain granularity within each benchmark. We draw on item compromise IRT \citep{eckerly2017,sinharay2017} and explanatory IRT \citep{deboeck2004}, adapting these frameworks through three structural modifications for LLM evaluation.
/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/paper_draft/method.tex-12-
artifacts/paper_draft/method.tex:13:\textit{Adaptation~1: Independent examinees $\to$ shared item pool.} Classical IRT assumes independent test-takers. In LLM evaluation, a fixed item set is shared across all models, and responses within a single model may exhibit residual correlations beyond the scalar~$\theta_j$ \citep{sanmartin2009}. We mitigate this by fitting per-benchmark models.
/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/paper_draft/method.tex-14-
artifacts/paper_draft/method.tex-15-\textit{Adaptation~2: Binary leakage $\to$ continuous exposure.} Item compromise IRT uses binary indicators \citep{sinharay2017}. LLM contamination is graded: a model may have encountered an item zero, once, or hundreds of times. We replace binary indicators with continuous exposure scores $e_{ij} \in [0,1]$ derived from detection signals, following the explanatory IRT tradition \citep{deboeck2004}.
